course: dictionary
lesson: 10995
title: Wary: careful and not too quick to trust
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Wary means careful and a little worried, because something might be dangerous or tricky. If you are wary of something, you watch it closely and do not trust it straight away. «Elephants are wary of bees, which can sting inside the trunk.» The elephant is not scared of bees for no reason. It knows they can hurt it, so it keeps its distance.
explain: Look at the words around wary to find its meaning. The sentence says bees can sting, so the elephant must be careful. Try your meaning in the sentence: «Elephants are careful of bees, which can sting inside the trunk» still makes sense, so wary means careful.
explain: Wary is often used with of, as in «be wary of» something or someone. A wary look shows that a person is watching for trouble. «Magpies are wary of shiny objects» means they watch shiny things carefully and do not simply trust them.
fact: Wary means careful and a little worried, because something might be dangerous or tricky.
fact: Wary can mean not quick to trust someone, as in «wary of strangers».
fact: Wary can also mean watchful, keeping an eye out for trouble.
fact: Red, yellow, orange and black often mean danger in nature, and «predators learn quickly or are born wary of them».
fact: Wary is an adjective, and it changes to warier and wariest when comparing.
exercise: «Elephants are wary of bees, which can sting inside the trunk.» What does wary mean here? | Bored of bees | Hungry for bees | Happy and playful around bees | *Careful, because bees can hurt them
exercise: Mia is told to be wary of emails that ask for passwords. What should she do? | Trust the email because it looks friendly | Reply quickly and give the password | Delete all her emails and stop using the computer | *Be careful and check before she answers
exercise: «Magpies are wary of shiny objects.» Find and copy the one word that means careful and watchful. | objects | magpies | shiny | *wary
test: The wary cat watched the dog from under the table. What does wary suggest? | The cat was chasing the dog around the room | The cat was asleep and did not notice the dog | The cat was happy to make friends straight away | *The cat was careful and kept watching for trouble
test: Which word fits best? The toddler was ___ of the hot oven and kept her hands back. | barely | *wary | glum | curt
exam: Which phrase shows someone is wary? | Laughing and running to hug the stranger at the gate | Shouting for the stranger to come in at once | Falling asleep on the bench while the stranger waited | *Keeping a wary eye on the stranger at the gate
